%S A399756 2,3,5,7,9,11,13,17,19,21,25,29,31,33,37,41,43,45,49,51,53,55,57,61,
%T A399756 65,67,71,73,79,81,85,89,91,93,97,101,103,105,109,111,113,117,121,127,
%U A399756 129,131,133,137,139,141,145,151,153,157,161,163,169,175,177,181,185,191,193,197,199
%N A399756 Numbers k such that 2^k-2 is a practical number.
%C A399756 In the theory of practical numbers, terms in this sequence are analogous to Mersenne prime exponents (A000043).
%C A399756 Every practical number greater than 2 must be divisible by either 4 or 6. Therefore, for 2^k-2 to be practical and divisible by 4, it requires 2^(k-1)-1 to be even which cannot happen if k > 1. Alternatively, for 2^k-2 to be practical and divisible by 6, 2^(k-1)-1 must be congruent to 0 (mod 3) and this requires k to be odd if k > 2. Consequently no practical number of the form 2^k-2 exists for k even except k = 2 and the first odd exponent that does not generate a practical number is 23.
%C A399756 Because 2^k-2 = 2*(2^(k-1)-1), it is double a Mersenne number with even exponent. Also every term in this sequence is the least member of a twin practical pair because 2^k will always be practical. Furthermore, sandwiched between these practical twins, 2^k-2 and 2^k are a subsets of Mersenne numbers and Mersenne primes including all Mersenne prime exponents up to 89. The first Mersenne prime not sandwiched between twin practical pairs is 107.
%e A399756 a(5) = 9, because 2^9-2 = 510 and 510 is practical.
%t A399756 PracticalQ[n_] := Module[{f, p, e, prod=1, ok=True}, If[n<1||(n>1&&OddQ[n]), False, If[n==1, True, f=FactorInteger[n]; {p, e}=Transpose[f]; Do[If[p[[i]]>1+DivisorSigma[1, prod], ok=False; Break[]]; prod=prod*p[[i]]^e[[i]], {i, Length[p]}]; ok]]]; Table[If[PracticalQ[2^n-2], n, Nothing], {n, 1, 200}]
%Y A399756 Cf. A000043, A005153.
%K A399756 nonn,new
%O A399756 1,1
